#, fuzzy
msgid "rtapi_get_time, rtapi_get_clocks - get the current time"
msgstr "Cambiar la vista actual"

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:15
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"long long rtapi_get_time();\n"
"long long rtapi_get_clocks();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:27
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*rtapi_get_time* returns the current time in nanoseconds. Depending on\n"
"the RTOS, this may be time since boot, or time since the clock period\n"
"was set, or some other time. Its absolute value means nothing, but it is\n"
"monotonically increasing and can be used to schedule future events, or\n"
"to time the duration of some activity. Returns a 64 bit value. The\n"
"resolution of the returned value may be as good as one nano-second, or\n"
"as poor as several microseconds. May be called from init/cleanup code,\n"
"and from within realtime tasks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:43
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*rtapi_get_clocks* returns the current time in CPU clocks. It is fast,\n"
"since it just reads the TSC in the CPU instead of calling a kernel or\n"
"RTOS function. Of course, times measured in CPU clocks are not as\n"
"convenient, but for relative measurements this works fine. Its absolute\n"
"value means nothing, but it is monotonically increasing and can be used\n"
"to schedule future events, or to time the duration of some activity. (On\n"
"SMP machines, the two TSC's may get out of sync, so if a task reads the\n"
"TSC, gets swapped to the other CPU, and reads again, the value may\n"
"decrease. RTAPI tries to force all RT tasks to run on one CPU.) Returns\n"
"a 64 bit value. The resolution of the returned value is one CPU clock,\n"
"which is usually a few nanoseconds to a fraction of a nanosecond. Note\n"
"that _long long_ math may be poorly supported on some platforms,\n"
"especially in kernel space. Also note that rtapi_print() will NOT print\n"
"__long long__s. Most time measurements are relative, and should be done\n"
"like this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "deltat = (long int)(end_time - start_time);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:54
msgid ""
"where end_time and start_time are longlong values returned from "
"rtapi_get_time, and deltat is an ordinary long int (32 bits). This will work "
"for times up to a second or so, depending on the CPU clock frequency. It is "
"best used for millisecond and microsecond scale measurements though."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:58
msgid "Returns the current time in nanoseconds or CPU clocks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:62
msgid ""
"Certain versions of the Linux kernel provide a global variable *cpu_khz*. "
"Computing"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "deltat = (end_clocks - start_clocks) / cpu_khz:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:69
msgid "gives the duration measured in milliseconds. Computing"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "deltat = (end_clocks - start_clocks) * 1000000 / cpu_khz:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:77
msgid ""
"gives the duration measured in nanoseconds for deltas less than about 9 "
"trillion clocks (e.g., 3000 seconds at 3 GHz)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man3/rtapi_get_time.3.adoc:81
msgid ""
