#. type: Title ==
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:95 src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "SPI CLOCK RATES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:101
msgid ""
"The maximum SPI clock of the BCM2835-SPI driver and the 7i90 is documented "
"over 32MHz. The SPI driver can provide frequencies well beyond what is "
"acceptable for the 7i90. A safe value to start with would be 12.5 MHz "
"(spiclk_rate=12500) and then work your way up from there."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:107
msgid ""
"The SPI driver generates (very) discrete clock frequency values, especially "
"in the MHz range because of a simple clock divider structure.  The base "
"frequency is 250 MHz and the divider for SPI0/SPI1 scales using discrete "
"factors. The following list specifies the *spiclk_rate* setting and the "
"discrete SPI clock frequency (250 MHz / (2n) for n > 1): - 62500"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:111
msgid ""
"62.500 MHz, - 41667 - 41.667 MHz, - 31250 - 31.250 MHz, - 25000 - 25.000 "
"MHz, - 20834 - 20.833 MHz, - 17858 - 17.857 MHz, - 15625 - 15.625 MHz, - "
"13889 - 13.889 MHz, - 12500 - 12.500 MHz, - 11364 - 11.364 MHz, - 10417 - "
"10.417 MHz, - 9616 - 9.615 MHz, - ...."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:117
msgid ""
"The lowest selectable SPI clock frequency is 30 kHz (spiclk_rate=30) for "
"SPI0 and SPI1. Theoretically, the SPI0 port could go slower, but there is no "
"point in doing so. You should not expect any real-time performance with such "
"slow setting, unless your machine is located next to a black hole."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:125
msgid ""
"The highest SPI clock frequency is, theoretically, 125 MHz. However, you "
"will not be able to build any reliable hardware interface at that frequency. "
"The driver limits the clock to 62.5 MHz (cpiclk_rate=62500).  The chances "
"are rather slim that you get the interface to work reliably at this "
"frequency. The 7i90 interface only supports frequencies up to 50 MHz and "
"that is with perfect cabling and impedance matching (in write direction "
"only)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:132
msgid ""
"Writing to the 7i90 may be done faster than reading. This is especially "
"important if you have \"long\" wires or any buffers on the SPI-bus path.  "
"You can set the read clock frequency to a lower value (using "
"*spiclk_rate_rd*) to counter the effects of the SPI-bus round-trip needed "
"for read actions. For example, you can write at 41.67 MHz and read at 25.00 "
"MHz."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:137
msgid ""
"It should be noted that the Rpi3 *must* have an adequate 5V power supply and "
"the power should be properly decoupled right on the 40-pin I/O header. At "
"high speeds and noise on the supply, there is the possibility of noise "
"throwing off the SoC's PLL(s), resulting in strange behaviour."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:143
msgid ""
"For optimal performance on the Rpi3, you must disable the \"ondemand\" CPU "
"frequency governor. You may add the following to your /etc/rc.local file: "
"echo -n 1200000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/scaling_min_freq "
"echo -n performance > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/"
"scaling_governor"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:146
msgid ""
"Be sure to have a proper heatsink mounted on the SoC or it will get too warm "
"and crash."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title =
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:1
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "hm2_spi(9)"
msgstr "hm2_pci"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:7
msgid ""
"hm2_spi - LinuxCNC HAL driver for the Mesa Electronics SPI Anything IO "
"boards, with HostMot2 firmware."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "*loadrt hm2_spi* [**config=\"**__str__[,__str__...]**\"** ] [ **spidev_path=**_path_[,_path_...] ] [**spidev_rate=**__rate__[,__rate__...] ]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spidev_path* [default: \"/dev/spidev1.0\"]"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:17
msgid "The path to the spi device node, a character special device in /dev"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spidev_rate* [default: 24000]"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:21
msgid ""
"The desired rate of the SPI clock in kHz. If the exact specified clock is "
"not available, a lower clock is used. Due to shortcomings in the spidev API, "
"it is not possible for hal to report the actual clock used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:27
msgid ""
"hm2_spi is a device driver that interfaces Mesa's SPI based Anything I/O "
"boards (with the HostMot2 firmware) to the LinuxCNC HAL."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:32
msgid ""
"The board must have a compatible firmware loaded on the board by the "
"mesaflash(1) program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:34
msgid ""
"hm2_spi is only available when LinuxCNC is configured with \"uspace\" "
"realtime."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:41
msgid ""
"It is possible for one SPI bus to connect several devices; in this "
"configuration, a master device has several chip select lines. In order to "
"meet realtime deadlines, hm2_spi should be used on a dedicated SPI interface "
"not shared with any other slaves."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "REALTIME PERFORMANCE OF LINUX SPIDEV DRIVERS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:49
msgid ""
"As of kernel 3.8, most or all kernel SPI drivers do not achieve the high "
"realtime response rate required for a typical LinuxCNC configuration.  The "
"driver was tested with a modified version of the spi-s3c64xx SPI driver on "
"the Odroid U3 platform. The patched kernel resides https://github.com/jepler/"
"odroid-linux/tree/odroid-3.8.13-rt[on github]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:54
msgid ""
"The maximum SPI clock of the 7i90 is documented as 50MHz. Other elements of "
"the data path between HAL and the 7i90 may impose other limitations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:6
msgid ""
"hostmot2 - LinuxCNC HAL driver for the Mesa Electronics HostMot2 firmware."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:11
msgid ""
"See the config modparam section below for Mesa card configuration.  "
"Typically hostmot2 is loaded with no parameters unless debugging is required."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:13
#, no-wrap
msgid "*loadrt hostmot2* [ **debug_idrom=**__N__ ] [ **debug_module_descriptors=**__N__ ] [ **debug_pin_descriptors=**__N__ ] [ **debug_modules=**__N__ ]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "*debug_idrom* [default: 0]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:16
msgid ""
"Developer/debug use only! Enable debug logging of the HostMot2 IDROM header."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "*debug_module_descriptors* [default: 0]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:18
msgid ""
"Developer/debug use only! Enables debug logging of the HostMot2 Module "
"Descriptors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "*debug_pin_descriptors* [default: 0]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:20
msgid ""
"Developer/debug use only! Enables debug logging of the HostMot2 Pin "
"Descriptors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "*debug_modules* [default: 0]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:22
msgid ""
"Developer/debug use only! Enables debug logging of the HostMot2 Modules used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:22
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "*use_serial_numbers* [default: 0]"
msgstr "*echo_serial_number*"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:30
msgid ""
"When creating HAL pins for smart-serial devices name the pins by the board "
"serial number rather than which board and port they are connected to. With "
"this option set to 1 pins will have names like *hm2_8i20.1234.current* "
"rather than *hm2_5i23.0.8i20.0.1.current*.  The identifier consists of the "
"last 4 digits of the board serial number, which is normally on a sticker on "
"the board. This will make configs less portable, but does mean that boards "
"can be re-connected less carefully."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:38
msgid ""
"hostmot2 is a device driver that interfaces the Mesa or litehm2 HostMot2 "
"firmware to the LinuxCNC HAL. This driver by itself does nothing, the boards "
"that actually run the firmware require their own drivers before anything can "
"happen. Currently drivers are available for PCI, Ethernet, SPI and EPP "
"interfaced cards."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:43
msgid ""
"The HostMot2 firmware provides modules such as encoders, PWM generators, "
"step/dir generators, and general purpose I/O pins (GPIOs). These things are "
"called \"Modules\". The firmware is configured, at firmware compile time, to "
"provide zero or more instances of each of these Modules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:44
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Board I/O Pins"
msgstr "or2 Pines"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:49
msgid ""
"The HostMot2 firmware runs on an FPGA board. The board interfaces with the "
"computer via PCI, Ethernet, SPI, or EPP, and interfaces with motion control "
"hardware, such as servos and stepper motors via I/O pins on the board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:55
msgid ""
"Each I/O pin can be configured, at board-driver load time, to serve one of "
"two purposes: Either as a particular I/O pin of a particular Module instance "
"(encoder, pwmgen, stepgen etc), or as a general purpose digital I/O pin. By "
"default all Module instances are enabled, and all the board's pins are used "
"by the Module instances."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:59
msgid ""
"The user can disable Module instances at board-driver load time, by "
"specifying a hostmot2 config string modparam. Any pins which belong to "
"Module instances that have been disabled automatically become GPIOs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:67
msgid ""
"All IO pins have some HAL presence, whether they belong to an active module "
"instance or are full GPIOs. GPIOs can be changed (at run-time)  between "
"inputs, normal outputs, and open drains, and have a flexible HAL interface. "
"IO pins that belong to active Module instances are constrained by the "
"requirements of the owning Module, and have a more limited interface in HAL. "
"This is described in the General Purpose I/O section below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "config modparam"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:75
msgid ""
"All the board-driver modules (hm2_pci, hm2_eth etc) accept a load-time "
"modparam of type string array, named \"config\". This array has one config "
"string for each board the driver should use. Each board's config string is "
"passed to and parsed by the hostmot2 driver when the board-driver registers "
"the board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:78
msgid ""
"The config string can contain spaces, so it is usually a good idea to wrap "
"the whole thing in double-quotes (the \" character)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:80
msgid ""
"The comma character (,) separates members of the config array from each "
"other."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:83
msgid ""
"For example, if your control computer has one 5I20 and one 5I23 you might "
"load the hm2_pci driver with a HAL command (in halcmd) something like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:86
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "*loadrt hm2_pci config=\"firmware=hm2/5i20/SVST8_4.BIT num_encoders=3 num_pwmgens=3 num_stepgens=3,firmware=hm2/5i23/SVSS8_8.BIT sserial_port_0=0000 num_encoders=4\"*\n"
msgstr "loadrt hm2_eth board_ip=10.10.10.10 config=\"num_encoders=2 num_pwmgens=2 num_stepgens=3\"\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:96
msgid ""
"Note: this assumes that the hm2_pci driver detects the 5I20 first and the "
"5I23 second.  If the detection order does not match the order of the config "
"strings, the hostmot2 driver will refuse to load the firmware and the board-"
"driver (hm2_pci etc) will fail to load. To the best of my knowledge, there "
"is no way to predict the order in which PCI boards will be detected by the "
"driver, but the detection order will be consistent as long as PCI boards are "
"not moved around. Best to try loading it and see what the detection order is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
