#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:89
msgid ""
"The default kinematics type is *KINEMATICS_IDENTITY*. GUIs may provide "
"special features for configurations using this default kinematics type.  For "
"instance, the AXIS GUI automatically handles joint and world mode operations "
"so that the distinctions between joints and axes are not visible to the "
"operator. This is feasible since there is an exact correspondence between a "
"joint number and its matching axis letter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "The kinematics type can be set with the *kinstype=* parameter"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "kinstype=**1** for KINEMATICS_IDENTITY (default if kinstype= omitted)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:98
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "kinstype=[**b**|**B**] for KINEMATICS_BOTH"
msgstr "KINEMATICS_FORWARD_ONLY"

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:101
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "kinstype=[**f**|**F**] for KINEMATICS_FORWARD_ONLY"
msgstr "KINEMATICS_FORWARD_ONLY"

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:104
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "kinstype=[**i**|**I**] for KINEMATICS_INVERSE_ONLY"
msgstr "KINEMATICS_INVERSE_ONLY"

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:105
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Example: loadrt *trivkins coordinates=xyz kinstype=b*"
msgstr ""
"[KINS]\n"
"KINEMATICS = trivkins coordinates=xyz kinstype=both\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:110
msgid ""
"Use kinstype=**B** (KINEMATICS_BOTH) for configurations that need to move "
"joints independently (joint mode) or as coordinated (teleop)  movements in "
"world coordinates."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:113
msgid ""
"When using the axis gui with KINEMATICS_BOTH, the '*$*' key is used to "
"toggle between joint and teleop (world) modes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:116
msgid ""
"An axis letter may be used more than once (*duplicated*) to assign multiple "
"joints to a single axis coordinate letter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:119
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Example: coordinates=**xyyzw** kinstype=**B**"
msgstr ""
"[KINS]\n"
"KINEMATICS = trivkins coordinates=xyz kinstype=both\n"

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "Assigns: x==joint0, y==joint1 *AND* joint2, z==joint3, w==joint4"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:131
msgid ""
"The above example illustrates a gantry configuration that uses *duplicated* "
"coordinate letters to indicate that two joints (joint1 and joint2) move a "
"single axis (y). Using kinstype=**B** allows the configuration to be toggled "
"between joint and world modes of operation.  Homing configuration options "
"are available to synchronize the final homing move for selected joints -- "
"see the documentation for *Homing Configuration*."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "*NOTES* for *duplicated* coordinates:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/kins.9.adoc:151
msgid ""
"When *duplicated* coordinate letters are used, specifying KINEMATICS_BOTH "
"(kinstype=**B**) allows a gui to support jogging of each individual joint in "
"*joint mode*. *Caution* is required for machines where the movement of a "
"single joint (in a set specified by a *duplicated* coordinate letter) can "
"lead to gantry racking or other unwanted outcomes. When the kinstype= "
"parameter is omitted, operation defaults to KINEMATICS_IDENTITY "
"(kinstype=**1**) and a gui may allow jogging based upon a selected axis "
"coordinate letter (or by a keyboard key) before homing is completed and the "
"machine is still in *joint mode*. The joint selected will depend upon the "
"gui implementation but typically only one of the multiple joints in the set "
"will jog.  Consequently, specifying KINEMATICS_BOTH is recommended as it "
"enables support for unambiguous, independent jogging of each individual "
"joint.  Machines that implement homing for all joints (including the "
"provisions for synchronizing the final homing move for multiple joints) may "
"be homed at machine startup and automatically switch to *world* mode where "
"per-coordinate jogging is available."
msgstr ""

