#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*lcd* takes format strings much like those used in C and many other\n"
"languages in the printf and scanf functions and their variants.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:51
msgid ""
"The component was written specifically to support the Mesa 7I73 pendant "
"controller, however, it may be of use streaming data to other character "
"devices and, as the output format mimics the ADM3 terminal format, it could "
"be used to stream data to a serial device. Perhaps even a genuine ADM3. The "
"strings contain a mixture of text values (which are displayed directly), "
"\"escaped\" formatting codes and numerical format descriptors.  For a "
"detailed description of formatting codes see: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Printf ."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:54
msgid ""
"The component can be configured to display an unlimited number of "
"differently-formatted pages, which may be selected with a HAL pin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:55
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "*Escaped codes*"
msgstr "Códigos remapeables"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:61
msgid ""
"`\\n` Inserts a clear-to-end, carriage return and line feed character.  This "
"will still linefeed and clear even if an automatic wrap has occurred (lcd "
"has no knowledge of the width of the lcd display).  To print in the "
"rightmost column it is necessary to allow the format to wrap and omit the "
"`\\n` code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:64
msgid ""
"`\\t` Inserts a tab (actually 4 spaces in the current version rather than a "
"true tab.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:68
msgid ""
"`\\`__NN__ inserts the character defined by the hexadecimal code __NN__.  As "
"the ',' character is used in the format string to separate LCD instances it "
"must be represented by `\\2C` in the format string (the decimal separator is "
"handled differently)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:70
msgid "`\\\\` Inserts a literal `\\`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Numerical formats*"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*lcd* differs slightly from the standard printf conventions. One\n"
"significant difference is that width limits are strictly enforced to\n"
"prevent the LCD display wrapping and spoiling the layout. The field\n"
"width includes the sign character so that negative numbers will often\n"
"have a smaller valid range than positive. Numbers that do not fit in\n"
"the specified width are displayed as a line of asterisks (**********).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:81
msgid ""
"Each format begins with a \"`%`\" symbol. (For a literal `%` use \"`%%`\").  "
"Immediately after the % the following modifiers may be used:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:84
#, fuzzy
msgid ""
"  \" \" (space) Pad the number to the specified width with spaces. This is "
"the default and is not strictly necessary."
msgstr ""
"es el número de un generador de señal específico (los números comenzar en 0)."

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:86
#, fuzzy
msgid "  \"0\" Pad the number to the specified width with the numeral 0."
msgstr ""
"es el número de un generador de señal específico (los números comenzar en 0)."

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:88
msgid ""
"  \"+\" Force display of a + symbol before positive numbers. This (like the"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:91
msgid ""
"sign) will appear immediately to the left of the digits for a space-padded "
"number and in the extreme left position for a 0-padded number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:99
msgid ""
"  \"1234567890\" A numerical entry (other than the leading 0 above) defines "
"the total number of characters to display including the decimal separator "
"and the sign. Whilst this number can be as many digits as required, the "
"maximum field width is 20 characters. The inherent precision of the "
"\"double\" data type means that more than 14 digits will tend to show errors "
"in the least significant digits. The integer data types will never fill more "
"than 10 decimal digits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:105
msgid ""
"  Following the width specifier should be the decimal specifier. This can "
"only be a full-stop character (.) as the comma (,) is used as the instance "
"separator. Currently lcd does not access the locale information to determine "
"the correct separator but the *decimal-separator* HAL parameter can be used "
"to choose any desired separator."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:109
msgid ""
"  Following the decimal separator should be a number that determines how "
"many places of decimals to display. This entry is ignored in the case of "
"integer formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:113
msgid ""
"All the above modifiers are optional, but to specify a decimal precision the "
"decimal point must precede the precision, e.g., as in \"%.3f\". The default "
"decimal precision is 4."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:115
#, fuzzy
msgid "The numerical formats supported are:"
msgstr "Lo relevante para nuestro pinout es:"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:123
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%f %F* (for example, %+09.3f): These create a floating-point type HAL\n"
"pin. The example would be displayed in a 9-character field, with 3\n"
"places of decimals, as a decimal separator, padded to the left with 0s\n"
"and with a sign displayed for both positive and negative. Conversely a\n"
"plain %f would be 6 digits of decimal, variable format width, with a\n"
"sign only shown for negative numbers. Both %f and %F create exactly the\n"
"same format.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%i %d* (For example %+ 4d): Creates a signed (s32) HAL pin. The example\n"
"would display the value at a fixed 4 characters, space padded, width\n"
"including the \"+\" giving a range of +999 to -999. %i and %d create\n"
"identical output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%u* (for example %08u): Creates an unsigned (u32) HAL pin. The example\n"
"would be a fixed 8 characters wide, padded with zeros.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:136
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%x, %X*: Creates an unsigned (u32) HAL pin and displays the value in\n"
"Hexadecimal. Both %x and %X display capital letters for digits ABCDEF. A\n"
"width may be specified, though the u32 HAL type is only 8 hex digits\n"
"wide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:139
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%o*: Creates an unsigned (u32) pin and displays the value in octal\n"
"representation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:144
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%c*: Creates a u32 HAL pin and displays the character corresponding to\n"
"the value of the pin. Values less than 32 (space) are suppressed. A\n"
"width specifier may be used, for example %20c might be used to create a\n"
"complete line of one character.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*%b*: This specifier has no equivalent in printf. It creates a bit\n"
"(boolean) type HAL pin. The b should be followed by two characters and\n"
"the display will show the first of these when the pin is true, and the\n"
"second when false. Note that the characters follow, not precede the \"b\",\n"
"unlike the case with other formats. The characters may be \"escaped\" Hex\n"
"values. For example \"%b\\FF \" will display a solid black block if true,\n"
"and a space if false and \"%b\\7F\\7E\" would display right-arrow for false\n"
"and left-arrow for true. An unexpected value of 'E' indicates a\n"
"formatting error.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Pages*: The page separator is the \"|\" (pipe) character (if the actual\n"
"character is needed then \\7C may be used). A \"Page\" in this context\n"
"refers to a separate format which may be displayed on the same display.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/lcd.9.adoc:164
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Instances*: The instance separator is the comma. This creates a\n"
"completely separate lcd instance, for example to drive a second lcd\n"
"display on the second 7I73. The use of comma to separate instances is\n"
"built in to the modparam reading code so not even escaped commas \"\\,\"\n"
"can be used. A comma may be displayed by using the \\2C sequence.\n"
msgstr ""

